00:00:55.546 --> 00:00:56.379
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:00:56.379 --> 00:00:57.212
- They're postmenopausal.

00:00:57.212 --> 00:00:58.045
- [Kelly] Yeah, yeah.

00:00:58.045 --> 00:00:58.878
- But you know what?

00:00:58.878 --> 00:01:00.536
- The world doesn't
care about them anymore.

00:01:00.536 --> 00:01:01.526
- I know.

00:01:01.526 --> 00:01:02.858
But they still have value.

00:01:02.858 --> 00:01:05.906
And they're still smart, lovely ladies.

00:01:05.906 --> 00:01:06.786
- [Kelly] They're relevant.
- Yes.

00:01:06.786 --> 00:01:08.494
- [Kelly] No, I have to ask this too,

00:01:08.494 --> 00:01:10.106
because I just moved here and I love it.

00:01:10.106 --> 00:01:11.704
- I'm so happy you love it.

00:01:11.704 --> 00:01:12.537
Your kids like it?

00:01:12.537 --> 00:01:13.587
- I love it, my kids love it.

00:01:13.587 --> 00:01:14.420
- Good.

00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:15.253
- [Kelly] We love it here.

00:01:15.253 --> 00:01:16.663
But you moved here and
you were pretty young.

00:01:16.663 --> 00:01:18.463
And what was that first move like?

00:01:18.463 --> 00:01:19.343
- I moved here.

00:01:19.343 --> 00:01:20.754
So I graduated from college.

00:01:20.754 --> 00:01:22.002
I worked at a Shakespeare festival.

00:01:22.002 --> 00:01:26.322
I was doing like summer stock
theater, you know, like,

00:01:26.322 --> 00:01:27.754
you know, cleaning the toilets

00:01:27.754 --> 00:01:29.452
and building the sets and all of this.

00:01:29.452 --> 00:01:30.810
- [Kelly] That's cool.

00:01:30.810 --> 00:01:32.522
- And the Georgia Shakespeare

00:01:32.522 --> 00:01:34.623
where I worked, there was
a musical there that went

00:01:34.623 --> 00:01:38.399
to a strip mall in Florida
and I went with the musical.

00:01:38.399 --> 00:01:39.839
And so I worked there.

00:01:39.839 --> 00:01:44.239
And then I came up here to
audition for Utah Shakespeare.

00:01:44.239 --> 00:01:46.155
You can tell I wanted to do like,

00:01:46.155 --> 00:01:46.988
- Yeah.

00:01:46.988 --> 00:01:48.231
- Be on stage.
- Yeah.

00:01:48.231 --> 00:01:50.370
- And when I got here, a friend said,

00:01:50.370 --> 00:01:51.562
"Hey, here's a backstage,

00:01:51.562 --> 00:01:52.740
you should go check this out."

00:01:52.740 --> 00:01:53.770
So I checked it out.

00:01:53.770 --> 00:01:54.690
I went on auditions.

00:01:54.690 --> 00:01:56.915
I got three jobs that first day as like.

00:01:56.915 --> 00:01:57.748
- Wow.

00:01:57.748 --> 00:01:58.899
- I think I just walked in and I was like,

00:01:58.899 --> 00:02:00.327
"Hi, my name's Jen."

00:02:00.327 --> 00:02:02.466
"I'm from West Virginia, I like to act."

00:02:02.466 --> 00:02:03.299
You know, I mean.

00:02:03.299 --> 00:02:04.132
- I wanna do this.

00:02:04.132 --> 00:02:04.965
- [Jennifer] They just kept being like,

00:02:04.965 --> 00:02:06.218
"Okay, let's see what you can do."

00:02:06.218 --> 00:02:07.939
So somebody said, "You
should meet with an agent."

00:02:07.939 --> 00:02:10.551
Anyway, I ended up living here.

00:02:10.551 --> 00:02:12.570
I was understudying a Turgenev play

00:02:12.570 --> 00:02:14.410
'A Month in the Country'
at the roundabout.

00:02:14.410 --> 00:02:15.243
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:02:15.243 --> 00:02:16.900
- And I lived on a woman's kitchen floor.

00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:18.780
A woman that I found
off the bulletin board

00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:19.991
at Actor's Equity.

00:02:19.991 --> 00:02:21.179
- As you do, as one does.

00:02:21.179 --> 00:02:23.180
- [Jennifer] And there was
a futon in my suitcase.

00:02:23.180 --> 00:02:24.013
- Yeah.

00:02:24.013 --> 00:02:24.846
- And like tucked into the corner.

00:02:24.846 --> 00:02:25.679
- [Kelly] I remember futons.

00:02:25.679 --> 00:02:26.630
- Yeah.
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:02:26.630 --> 00:02:27.463
- And I lived there for nine months.

00:02:27.463 --> 00:02:28.296
- A couch and a bed.

00:02:28.296 --> 00:02:29.759
How awful is that?

00:02:29.759 --> 00:02:31.039
- I never folded it up.

00:02:31.039 --> 00:02:31.872
- [Kelly] Oh.

00:02:31.872 --> 00:02:32.705
- It was just a little
mattress in the corner.

00:02:32.705 --> 00:02:33.538
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:02:33.538 --> 00:02:34.864
No, I lived on a mattress
in someone's house in L.A.

00:02:34.864 --> 00:02:35.697
- [Jennifer] Yeah.

00:02:35.697 --> 00:02:36.530
- That was kind of similar.

00:02:36.530 --> 00:02:37.413
I think when you have a big dream,

00:02:37.413 --> 00:02:39.836
you just are willing to accept
whatever comes your way.

00:02:39.836 --> 00:02:40.669
Like to get there, yeah.

00:02:40.669 --> 00:02:41.876
- It didn't even phase me.

00:02:41.876 --> 00:02:42.709
- Wait, honestly?

00:02:42.709 --> 00:02:43.542
- I was thrilled.

00:02:43.542 --> 00:02:44.375
- No, me too.

00:02:44.375 --> 00:02:45.208
I was excited, that, is funny.

00:02:45.208 --> 00:02:46.041
Looking back,

00:02:46.041 --> 00:02:47.524
I'm just like, what in the world?

00:02:47.524 --> 00:02:49.215
Like if my kid was
like, I'm gonna do this.

00:02:49.215 --> 00:02:50.048
- Yeah.

00:02:50.048 --> 00:02:52.335
- But I, at the time was just
like, living my best life.

00:02:52.335 --> 00:02:54.146
- Yeah, it did not even occur to me

00:02:54.146 --> 00:02:55.175
- to feel weird,
- That it was weird.

00:02:55.175 --> 00:02:56.008
- That I was living.

00:02:56.008 --> 00:02:57.055
No, I was thrilled.

00:02:57.055 --> 00:02:58.031
- [Kelly] Yeah.
- And I don't think,

00:02:58.031 --> 00:02:59.874
I think we get too
precious about things now.

00:02:59.874 --> 00:03:00.707
- Yeah.

00:03:00.707 --> 00:03:01.884
- [Jennifer] And I had no money.

00:03:01.884 --> 00:03:04.535
I couldn't afford the subway
token to get to Times Square.

00:03:04.535 --> 00:03:05.836
I walked back and forth.

00:03:05.836 --> 00:03:06.669
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:03:06.669 --> 00:03:07.754
- I made $150 a week

00:03:07.754 --> 00:03:10.204
and I worked too much to do
real shifts at the restaurant.

00:03:10.204 --> 00:03:11.037
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:03:11.037 --> 00:03:13.735
- And somehow I just
survived, I just made it work.

00:03:13.735 --> 00:03:14.756
- [Kelly] Yeah.
- Yeah.

00:03:14.756 --> 00:03:15.589
- That's awesome.

00:03:15.589 --> 00:03:16.804
I love people hearing that too,

00:03:16.804 --> 00:03:17.916
because you're so successful.

00:03:17.916 --> 00:03:19.434
So those people killing it right now.

00:03:19.434 --> 00:03:21.295
Like there is light at
the end of the tunnel,

00:03:21.295 --> 00:03:22.154
you know, if you really put any time.

00:03:22.154 --> 00:03:23.105
Well, you know what I think is,

00:03:23.105 --> 00:03:24.996
enjoying the journey of it.

00:03:24.996 --> 00:03:26.453
And letting yourself be where you are

00:03:26.453 --> 00:03:28.172
not just looking ahead, looking ahead.

00:03:28.172 --> 00:03:29.527
Like, I'll be happy when,

00:03:29.527 --> 00:03:31.447
I'll be happy when I
get into this college,

00:03:31.447 --> 00:03:34.186
or when I am married or when this happens,

00:03:34.186 --> 00:03:36.203
or I must whatever.

00:03:36.203 --> 00:03:39.106
I just was like pretty
psyched to be right there.

00:03:39.106 --> 00:03:42.276
- So, whenever, whenever you
were talking about Shakespeare,

00:03:42.276 --> 00:03:44.855
I heard, is that the cast
that you did laundry for?

00:03:44.855 --> 00:03:45.688
- Is that, did you lose a bet?

00:03:45.688 --> 00:03:46.706
- Oh gosh.

00:03:46.706 --> 00:03:48.474
- Like I was like, you
did, you did laundry.

00:03:48.474 --> 00:03:50.904
- Okay, so in the process of all of this,

00:03:50.904 --> 00:03:52.754
when I worked at this strip mall.

00:03:52.754 --> 00:03:53.751
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:03:53.751 --> 00:03:57.866
- I was non-equity, but I
was an out of town person

00:03:57.866 --> 00:04:00.625
and I think I was making just
like a hundred dollars a week.

00:04:00.625 --> 00:04:01.458
- Oh.

00:04:01.458 --> 00:04:02.625
- But it was, I was professionally acting.

00:04:02.625 --> 00:04:04.055
- Yeah.
- You know what I mean?

00:04:04.055 --> 00:04:06.603
- And I couldn't have
another job at the same time.

00:04:06.603 --> 00:04:09.481
And so the owner of the theater, he said,

00:04:09.481 --> 00:04:11.081
"Well, what are you gonna,
what are you gonna do?"

00:04:11.081 --> 00:04:12.824
"How are you gonna afford
to live down here?"

00:04:12.824 --> 00:04:13.657
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:04:13.657 --> 00:04:14.529
- And I said, "I can do anything."

00:04:14.529 --> 00:04:17.419
"I can clean, I can do laundry."

00:04:17.419 --> 00:04:18.427
- That's why you're successful.

00:04:18.427 --> 00:04:20.219
You accepted any, like, I can work for it.

00:04:20.219 --> 00:04:22.197
- Oh, it didn't even, I
just said, I can do laundry.

00:04:22.197 --> 00:04:24.369
So there were 35 people in the cast,

00:04:24.369 --> 00:04:26.198
most, 35 men in the cast.

00:04:26.198 --> 00:04:29.419
And I washed and ironed
all 35 dress shirts

00:04:29.419 --> 00:04:31.957
between every show, eight shows a week.

00:04:31.957 --> 00:04:33.289
And I did that the whole time.

00:04:33.289 --> 00:04:35.078
And he let me be a roommate

00:04:35.078 --> 00:04:37.326
to another person in
one of the apartments.

00:04:37.326 --> 00:04:38.159
- [Kelly] That's amazing.

00:04:38.159 --> 00:04:38.992
- [Jennifer] Yeah.

00:04:38.992 --> 00:04:40.198
- I'm glad people are hearing that too.

00:04:40.198 --> 00:04:41.486
I was, 'cause you know why I,

00:04:41.486 --> 00:04:43.337
whenever I run into, especially musicians,

00:04:43.337 --> 00:04:44.344
- I can really iron a shirt a shirt.

00:04:44.344 --> 00:04:46.264
- No, because I run
into certain musicians,

00:04:46.264 --> 00:04:48.197
like, you know, throughout my
career and they're just like,

00:04:48.197 --> 00:04:49.717
oh, I don't, I can't get a job.

00:04:49.717 --> 00:04:51.976
I'm like, there's a Starbucks
right down the road,

00:04:51.976 --> 00:04:53.136
like there's a Target, right.

00:04:53.136 --> 00:04:53.969
Like, what are you doing?

00:04:53.969 --> 00:04:55.936
And they're too proud to go get a job

00:04:55.936 --> 00:04:57.246
that they think is beneath them.

00:04:57.246 --> 00:04:59.278
And I'm like, do you know
where all I've worked?

00:04:59.278 --> 00:05:00.111
- [Jennifer] Yeah.

00:05:00.111 --> 00:05:01.725
- Like, do you know
how many jobs I've had?

00:05:01.725 --> 00:05:04.525
- I just, I feel lucky
that that wasn't in my way.

00:05:04.525 --> 00:05:05.358
I didn't get in my own way,

00:05:05.358 --> 00:05:06.774
- It wasn't in mine either.

00:05:06.774 --> 00:05:08.056
- [Jennifer] And I
thought it was hilarious.

00:05:08.056 --> 00:05:11.656
I mean, by the way, can I just
be real here for a second?

00:05:11.656 --> 00:05:15.397
The amount of times I
sent 35 men on stage,

00:05:15.397 --> 00:05:19.467
in slightly damp, very wrinkled shirts.

00:05:19.467 --> 00:05:21.086
- [Kelly] Throw it in a
dryer with a damp towel.

00:05:21.086 --> 00:05:22.086
- A crease at the wrong place.

00:05:22.086 --> 00:05:23.838
I mean, I was just like, sorry.

00:05:23.838 --> 00:05:25.921
I mean, I was 22.
- Yeah.

00:05:26.859 --> 00:05:27.755
I don't think I could do it now.

00:05:27.755 --> 00:05:29.382
I don't iron well, I'm never ironed.

00:05:29.382 --> 00:05:31.424
Girl, I throw it in the
dryer with a damp towel.

00:05:31.424 --> 00:05:34.784
Anyway, so you, gets
the wrinkle right out.

00:05:34.784 --> 00:05:36.695
That was the look at her
new movie, 'Family Switch.'

00:05:36.695 --> 00:05:38.544
You can catch it now on Netflix.

00:05:38.544 --> 00:05:40.138
So explain, the movie's hilarious.

00:05:40.138 --> 00:05:40.971
Explain to everybody what's going on.

00:05:40.971 --> 00:05:41.804
- [Jennifer] Thank you,

00:05:41.804 --> 00:05:42.880
the movie is so much fun.

00:05:42.880 --> 00:05:45.690
It's really just, you know,
like all body switch movies,

00:05:45.690 --> 00:05:48.120
it's about walking a mile
in someone else's shoes.

00:05:48.120 --> 00:05:51.211
It's impossible to know what
your kids are going through,

00:05:51.211 --> 00:05:52.579
what your parents are going through,

00:05:52.579 --> 00:05:55.059
unless you can actually be them for a day.

00:05:55.059 --> 00:05:55.892
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:05:55.892 --> 00:05:56.919
- [Jennifer] And it's, you
know, just a lot of fun.

00:05:56.919 --> 00:06:00.043
Just in this case, we all switch.

00:06:00.043 --> 00:06:01.594
Ed switches with Brady,

00:06:01.594 --> 00:06:03.216
who plays our son, Brady Noon.

00:06:03.216 --> 00:06:04.314
Ed Helms, Ed Helms.

00:06:04.314 --> 00:06:05.147
- [Kelly] Yeah, love Ed.

00:06:05.147 --> 00:06:06.246
Yeah, we love him.

00:06:06.246 --> 00:06:08.464
- [Jennifer] The baby
and the dog switch too.

00:06:08.464 --> 00:06:10.635
Just to add a little chaos.

00:06:10.635 --> 00:06:11.815
So it's the most,

00:06:11.815 --> 00:06:13.585
- That's my favorite part.

00:06:13.585 --> 00:06:15.726
- And it has a little
bit of a holiday feel.

00:06:15.726 --> 00:06:18.465
It's the most fun, heartwarming, funny.

00:06:18.465 --> 00:06:20.447
If you have Ed Helms in
a movie, you're golden.

00:06:20.447 --> 00:06:24.047
And by the way, these kids
outshine the two of us,

00:06:24.047 --> 00:06:26.057
they are amazing.

00:06:26.057 --> 00:06:26.890
Amazing.

00:06:26.890 --> 00:06:27.723
- I love that.

00:06:27.723 --> 00:06:28.692
- [Jennifer] Yeah.
(audience applauds)

00:06:28.692 --> 00:06:30.103
- Do you love playing moms?

00:06:30.103 --> 00:06:30.936
Because I've seen you in a

00:06:30.936 --> 00:06:31.825
lot of movies where you're playing a mom.

00:06:31.825 --> 00:06:32.964
Do you love playing the mom role?

00:06:32.964 --> 00:06:33.797
- I do.

00:06:33.797 --> 00:06:34.630
- [Kelly] Because you do it well.

00:06:34.630 --> 00:06:35.505
- I really do, yeah.
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:06:35.505 --> 00:06:37.364
- I must be working something out

00:06:37.364 --> 00:06:39.305
because I keep playing mother, daughter.

00:06:39.305 --> 00:06:40.138
- Yeah.

00:06:40.138 --> 00:06:40.971
- [Jennifer] Yeah.

00:06:40.971 --> 00:06:41.804
- No, it's awesome though.

00:06:41.804 --> 00:06:43.292
We are, I'm telling you,
my family is obsessed.

00:06:43.292 --> 00:06:45.404
Like 'Yes Day', I'm telling

00:06:45.404 --> 00:06:47.443
that movie changed my
whole family dynamic.

00:06:47.443 --> 00:06:48.905
Like, literally they're
like, can we have a yes day?

00:06:48.905 --> 00:06:50.135
We've had so many yes days.

00:06:50.135 --> 00:06:50.968
- Good.

00:06:50.968 --> 00:06:52.683
So, well this is the same producing team.

00:06:52.683 --> 00:06:54.985
We started this process while we were on

00:06:54.985 --> 00:06:56.345
set making 'Yes day'.

00:06:56.345 --> 00:06:57.716
We were just like,

00:06:57.716 --> 00:06:58.924
oh, what else can we can,

00:06:58.924 --> 00:07:00.065
what else can we do?

00:07:00.065 --> 00:07:03.865
It's also loosely comes from
an Amy Krouse Rosenthal book,

00:07:03.865 --> 00:07:05.796
which she, bless her soul,

00:07:05.796 --> 00:07:07.384
also wrote 'Yes Day'.

00:07:07.384 --> 00:07:08.745
This was 'Bedtime for Mommy',

00:07:08.745 --> 00:07:11.135
about a little girl
putting her mom to bed,

00:07:11.135 --> 00:07:13.356
her parents to bed, and
they don't wanna go to bed.

00:07:13.356 --> 00:07:15.055
And they're, we want
another glass of water.

00:07:15.055 --> 00:07:15.888
We want this.

00:07:15.888 --> 00:07:18.265
So it's just kind of that switching idea.

00:07:18.265 --> 00:07:19.098
- Yeah.

00:07:19.098 --> 00:07:19.964
I love it.

00:07:19.964 --> 00:07:22.065
Yeah, my kids are gonna be
obsessed with this one too.

00:07:22.065 --> 00:07:22.898
